<!-- Pose Studio IK handles (#530): the bone chains that get a draggable handle.
       joints   root -> end. Every joint before the last is solved, the last one keeps
                (or takes) the handle's orientation.
                The handle sits where the WORN RIGGED MESH ends the limb: of the joints at
                and below the end joint that a worn rigged mesh weights vertices to, the
                one whose vertex region reaches farthest from the chain's root, at that
                region's far end (so a hoof's handle is on the hoof even when the wrist or
                ankle is the "knee"). Joint-position overrides and scale are included. The
                choice is logged at INFO ("IK chain ...") when Pose Studio shows the handles
                and whenever the outfit's rigging changes.
       tip      fallback when nothing worn is rigged at / below the end joint (system
                avatar, mesh still loading): the first of these joints the avatar has.
       hand_branches, hand_depth   a hand: when at least hand_branches children of the end
                joint carry rigged fingers, the search stops hand_depth joints below it, so
                a human hand's handle sits at the knuckles instead of a fingertip. A hoof
                rigged on the hand bones uses fewer branches and is not capped.
       hand_skip   a hand: joints whose name contains this (the thumb) never set the handle
                point, and the handle is attached to the end joint (the wrist), not a finger
       rigged   1: only shown when a worn rigged mesh binds the chain (at or below it)
       group    wings: shown by "IK wings" (SLPoseStudioIKWings, default on) instead of
                the Bento setting
       solver   twobone  a limb with an elbow / knee (pole) handle on its second joint:
                         analytic two-bone IK for 3 joints, FABRIK (every joint folds,
                         the pole turns the bend plane) when the chain is longer or has
                         grown to its hoof (extend). Only the root keeps its dial ranges
                         under "IK limits"; the hinges are capped by max_bend.
                ccd      tail, wings, ears: FABRIK (analytic for 3 joints) with every joint
                         held to its dial ranges, then damped CCD to close what the limits
                         allow
                lookat   aim the last joint's forward axis at the handle, the joints
                         before it share part of the turn (neck + head)
                reach    (#1566) the joints plus the tip (the nose) as one FABRIK chain:
                         pulling the handle on the nose bends the neck so the nose reaches
                         the point; Ctrl / the rings turn the head in place
                gaze     (#1566) a look-at point: the eyes turn first, up to their limit,
                         then the head, then the neck (limits)
                pelvis   the root diamond, under the hips: move / turn the pelvis itself;
                         the other chains' targets come along with "IK targets follow the
                         hips" on (SLPoseStudioIKFollowRoot), else they stay and re-solve
                hips     (#1566) the hips box: moves / turns the pelvis; planted chains
                         (and pinned ones) stay where they are and re-solve, the rest follow
       shape    box: drawn as a box (hips, chest). A box ccd chain (the chest) carries the
                chains below its end (arms, head) like the hips do
       planted  1: stays put when the hips box moves (legs, hind legs)
       eye_sets gaze only: the eye bones it can turn, one set picked by "Eye IK" in Pose Studio
                (SLPoseStudioIKEyeBones): eyes (mEyeLeft / Right), alt (mFaceEyeAlt*, Bento mesh
                heads), nose (mFaceNoseLeft / Right: the Open Pony rig's eyes), both (eyes + alt).
                Auto picks by what the worn rigged meshes weight: alt (with eyes too: both),
                else eyes, else nose, else eyes. forward: the set's aim axis in its parent's
                frame (every SL bone binds unrotated, so +X, the way the avatar faces)
                type pupil (#1567): eyes that look by SLIDING the bone over the eye (the Open
                Pony's pupils): a position offset from the bone's rest, and (model "shell") a
                turn that keeps the pupil lying flat on the eye. Everything is in the bone's
                parent frame (+X forward, +Y left, +Z up), metres, relative to the bone's rest
                position. "pupil" maps each joint to:
                  model "shell"    the Open Pony dev kit's own eye-tracking rig (below):
                    pivot, arm       an arm of this length pivoting here aims at the target
                    aim_offset       ... plus this offset (the rig's per-eye target offset),
                    aim_center       turned by the look's yaw as seen from this point
                    shell_center, shell_axes   the eye shell, an ellipsoid (its centre and three
                                     semi-axis vectors at right angles; or shell_radius: a
                                     sphere): the pupil is the point of its front nearest the
                                     arm's tip
                    axis             the bone's rest axis (head -> tail): turned onto the
                                     shell's normal there (absent: never turned)
                  model "linear"   (default) for other rigs, from gains:
                    h_axis, h_sign   the axis (x / y / z) the pupil moves along for a look to
                                     the left, and which way (+1 / -1); v_axis, v_sign: look up
                    gain_h, gain_v   metres per degree of look
                    range            [h, v]: the ellipse the pupil stays in
                    vergence         extra inward offset in metres at 1 m, scaled by 1 / distance
                    inward           which way along h_axis is toward the nose (+1 / -1)
                  center           [x, y, z]: added to the result (a calibrated rest offset)
                The look is held within the eye limit (Pose Studio) / eye range (look-at) of
                the set's forward. Each eye measures the target from its own place.
                THE OPEN PONY ("nose" set), read from the dev kit V3.2 ([OP] OpenPony Mesh and
                Rigging.blend, same rig in Animations Full.blend) with scripts/tools/
                blend_rig_dump.py. FaceNoseLeft / Right (the controls mFaceNose* copy in world
                space, location and rotation) carry "Copy Location" of IKEyeLeft / Right's TAIL
                (enabled by a driver when the IKEyeTrackDriver slider > 0.1) then "Shrinkwrap"
                (nearest surface point, distance 0, "Align to Normal" on the bone's Y axis) onto
                the OBIKEye mesh (one shell per eye; the bind pupil disc sits 0 .. 0.65 mm over
                it, ~1.9 mm over the eye white OBEye). IKEyeLeft: head (pivot) Blender (0.06933, -0.28784,
                0.90820), 55.87 mm long, Damped Track to IKEyeTargetLeft; IKEyecenter (its
                parent) is Child Of Head, at Blender (0, -0.27020, 0.92073). The targets are
                children of IKEyefocus (which Locked + Damped Tracks IKEyecenter, so it turns
                with the look), offset +-91.93 mm along its side axis, so the eyes aim at focus
                +- 91.93 mm: parallel, no convergence. The bind pupil (mFaceNoseLeft head) is
                Blender (0.09209, -0.34602, 0.91801), tail (0.10370, -0.35835, 0.91801);
                Blender -> SL is (x, y, z) -> (-y, x, z). So, SL, relative to the bind pupil
                (left eye; the right mirrors y): pivot (-58.18, -22.76, -9.81) mm, aim offset
                (0, +91.93, 0) mm, aim centre (-75.82, -92.09, 2.72) mm, axis (0.7281, 0.6856, 0).
                The shell's front is an ellipsoid to 0.036 mm (algebraic fit of the OBIKEye
                vertices within 60 mm of the straight-look pupil: all the eye range reaches):
                centre (-26.38, -19.56, 0.43) mm, semi-axes 124.80 mm (vertical), 93.59 mm
                (along the eye), 32.31 mm (outward). The rig's straight look puts the pupil at
                (+10.3, -12.1, -10.1) mm (left), turned ~3.5 degrees from its bind; looks move
                it ~0.5 mm per degree sideways (yaw: along (-0.7, +0.7, 0) for the left eye,
                mirrored on the right, so both slide the same way) and ~1 mm per degree up /
                down. Measured against the rig on its own mesh (yaw / pitch to 45 degrees, both
                eyes): the pupil within 0.3 mm of the rig's and of the shell, its turn within
                0.1 degree, the pupil disc's gap over the eye white within the rig's own -0.30
                .. +0.17 mm of the bind. The turn matters: the pupils are curved discs ~80 mm
                wide; slid without it, one side cut 2.4 mm into the eye white and the other
                lifted 3 mm at 30 degrees. The kit's keyed eye animations (eyesL / eyesR
                layers, shrinkwrapped the same way) sit at (+14.5, -17.1, 0) mm most often and
                span about 25 mm. Cross-check in world: SLEyeCaptureLog +
                scripts/tools/eye_capture_summary.py.
       limits   gaze only: cone limits in degrees, eyes first, then the joints tip -> root
       tip_length  reach only: the nose's distance in front of the head when no tip joint
                exists (metres, scaled with the avatar)
       mirror   the opposite chain (mirror mode drags it mirrored; just for display)
       color    RGB of the handle
       bend     twobone only: where the lower bone swings when a straight limb starts to
                bend, in the root joint's frame (forearm forward: +X; shin back: -X); the
                elbow / knee handle (pole) overrides it once dragged
       extend   when the handle point (worn mesh) lies on a joint below the chain's end
                and the end is not a hand (see hand_branches), the chain grows by up to
                this many joints toward it: a pony's hoof below the wrist / cannon becomes
                the end bone (it takes the handle's orientation) and the wrist / cannon
                folds with the elbow and shoulder. 0 / absent: never (human legs: the
                foot is not a limb segment).
       max_bend twobone: how far a hinge may fold, degrees from straight (default 160)
       bento    1: an optional Bento chain (tail, ears, hind limbs), only shown with
                "Bento IK handles" (SLPoseStudioIKBentoChains) on
       forward  lookat only: the aiming axis in the last joint's frame (avatars face +X)
       share    lookat only: the part of the turn each joint before the last takes
     Chains whose joints the avatar does not have are skipped. Data-driven so pony /
     quadruped and human skeletons both work: edit or add chains here. -->
